= Confirmation bias =

== Overview ==
'''Confirmation bias''' is a family of tendencies to seek, generate, interpret, test, weight, or remember information in ways that favor an existing belief or hypothesis.

It is not one single mechanism.

== Information search ==
People may preferentially look for evidence that would be expected if their current hypothesis were true.

A positive test strategy can sometimes be useful, but can become misleading when disconfirming tests would be more diagnostic.

== Interpretation ==
Ambiguous evidence can be interpreted in ways consistent with prior beliefs.

The same information can therefore appear stronger to people starting from different assumptions.

== Memory and weighting ==
People may remember congruent information more readily or discount conflicting evidence.

These effects depend on motivation, prior knowledge, emotional investment, task structure, and evidence quality.

== Confirmation bias and dishonesty ==
Confirmation bias does not require conscious deception.

A person can sincerely evaluate evidence in a biased way.

Deliberate cherry-picking and strategic advocacy can produce similar patterns but are different explanations.

== Science ==
Scientific methods use preregistration, blinding, adversarial tests, replication, and explicit alternatives partly because individual judgment is vulnerable to selective search and interpretation.

These methods reduce some pathways to confirmation but do not make scientists bias-free.

== References ==
* Raymond S. Nickerson, “Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many Guises” (1998).
* hypothesis-testing and motivated-reasoning literature.

